WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:05.894
Two masses sit between two walls

2
00:00:05.894 --> 00:00:07.980
held by three springs.

3
00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:11.700
Their stiffnesses are two, one, and two.

4
00:00:12.050 --> 00:00:14.262
A force of four pushes the

5
00:00:14.347 --> 00:00:16.388
first mass to the right.

6
00:00:16.388 --> 00:00:18.770
Where do the masses settle?

7
00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:22.078
We could write the force equations.

8
00:00:22.078 --> 00:00:25.037
Or we could ask nature's question

9
00:00:25.037 --> 00:00:27.403
where is the energy lowest?

10
00:00:27.403 --> 00:00:28.840
Pause and think.

11
00:00:32.420 --> 00:00:34.826
Start with one variable.

12
00:00:34.826 --> 00:00:38.336
The parabola one half k x squared

13
00:00:38.336 --> 00:00:42.547
minus x f, has its lowest point where its

14
00:00:42.647 --> 00:00:45.254
slope k x minus f is zero

15
00:00:45.254 --> 00:00:47.460
at x equals f over k.

16
00:00:47.810 --> 00:00:50.507
For a symmetric matrix K

17
00:00:50.614 --> 00:00:53.527
the energy is P of x equals

18
00:00:53.635 --> 00:00:56.331
one half x transpose K x

19
00:00:56.331 --> 00:00:58.596
minus x transpose f.

20
00:00:58.596 --> 00:01:03.450
Its level curves are rings around one point.

21
00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:06.651
Its gradient is K x minus f

22
00:01:06.651 --> 00:01:09.604
and its Hessian is K itself.

23
00:01:09.705 --> 00:01:13.065
So the flat point of P is exactly

24
00:01:13.167 --> 00:01:16.120
the solution of K x equals f.

25
00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:18.966
When K is positive definite

26
00:01:18.966 --> 00:01:22.284
that flat point is the bottom of a bowl.

27
00:01:22.365 --> 00:01:25.198
Solving K x equals f and minimising

28
00:01:25.279 --> 00:01:27.140
P are the same problem.

29
00:01:27.490 --> 00:01:31.016
Even better, completing the square gives

30
00:01:31.104 --> 00:01:32.691
an exact identity.

31
00:01:32.779 --> 00:01:35.247
The extra energy of any x is

32
00:01:35.335 --> 00:01:38.068
one half of its error, times K

33
00:01:38.068 --> 00:01:41.770
times its error: the energy of the error.

34
00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:45.872
For our springs, K is three, minus one

35
00:01:45.872 --> 00:01:49.624
minus one, three, and f is four, zero.

36
00:01:49.624 --> 00:01:52.895
The minimum is at one point five

37
00:01:52.895 --> 00:01:56.840
zero point five, where P is minus three.

38
00:01:57.190 --> 00:01:59.986
Every other point is higher.

39
00:01:59.986 --> 00:02:01.884
One, zero and two

40
00:02:01.884 --> 00:02:05.479
one both give minus two point five.

41
00:02:05.479 --> 00:02:09.574
One point four, zero point six is close

42
00:02:09.574 --> 00:02:12.470
at minus two point nine six.

43
00:02:13.050 --> 00:02:16.529
So is the solution of K x equals f

44
00:02:16.631 --> 00:02:20.008
always the minimum of the energy?

45
00:02:20.008 --> 00:02:21.850
Pause and decide.

46
00:02:25.430 --> 00:02:29.512
Take Lecture twenty two's matrix one, three

47
00:02:29.512 --> 00:02:30.626
three, one

48
00:02:30.626 --> 00:02:34.059
with eigenvalues four and minus two

49
00:02:34.059 --> 00:02:36.564
and f equal to four, four.

50
00:02:36.564 --> 00:02:38.976
The solution is one, one

51
00:02:38.976 --> 00:02:41.110
where P is minus four.

52
00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:44.830
But walk from there along one, minus one.

53
00:02:44.830 --> 00:02:47.789
P drops to minus six, minus twelve

54
00:02:47.789 --> 00:02:50.831
minus twenty two, and keeps falling.

55
00:02:50.831 --> 00:02:52.968
The solution is a saddle.

56
00:02:52.968 --> 00:02:55.516
Without positive definiteness

57
00:02:55.516 --> 00:02:57.900
there is no minimum to find.

58
00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:02.449
Where did the springs' K come from?

59
00:03:02.449 --> 00:03:05.624
Three steps, each a matrix.

60
00:03:05.624 --> 00:03:07.439
First, stretch

61
00:03:07.439 --> 00:03:10.954
spring one stretches by x one

62
00:03:10.954 --> 00:03:14.697
spring two by x two minus x one

63
00:03:14.697 --> 00:03:17.985
spring three by minus x two.

64
00:03:17.985 --> 00:03:20.480
That is e equals A x.

65
00:03:20.830 --> 00:03:23.054
Second, Hooke's law

66
00:03:23.166 --> 00:03:26.280
the tension is the stiffness

67
00:03:26.391 --> 00:03:29.950
times the stretch, w equals C e.

68
00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:33.207
Third, balance: at each mass

69
00:03:33.207 --> 00:03:36.815
the spring forces balance the load

70
00:03:36.815 --> 00:03:39.220
A transpose w equals f.

71
00:03:39.570 --> 00:03:43.498
Together, A transpose C A x equals f.

72
00:03:43.498 --> 00:03:48.275
That product is three, minus one, minus one

73
00:03:48.275 --> 00:03:49.018
three.

74
00:03:49.124 --> 00:03:52.415
It is positive definite because

75
00:03:52.521 --> 00:03:55.281
A has independent columns

76
00:03:55.281 --> 00:03:58.890
and the stiffnesses are positive.

77
00:03:59.470 --> 00:04:00.616
At the minimum

78
00:04:00.616 --> 00:04:03.441
the first mass moves one point five

79
00:04:03.517 --> 00:04:05.503
and pulls the second along

80
00:04:05.579 --> 00:04:07.030
by zero point five.

81
00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:10.923
Spring one is stretched, with tension three.

82
00:04:10.923 --> 00:04:13.983
Springs two and three are compressed

83
00:04:13.983 --> 00:04:15.915
with tension minus one.

84
00:04:15.915 --> 00:04:18.572
The balance gives back the load

85
00:04:18.572 --> 00:04:19.780
four and zero.

86
00:04:20.130 --> 00:04:23.775
The energy stored in the springs is three

87
00:04:23.775 --> 00:04:27.072
and the work done by the load is six.

88
00:04:27.072 --> 00:04:30.370
So P is three minus six, minus three.

89
00:04:30.370 --> 00:04:31.759
At the minimum

90
00:04:31.759 --> 00:04:35.490
the stored energy is always half the work.

91
00:04:36.070 --> 00:04:38.960
Why minimise, if we can solve?

92
00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:42.717
Because a minimum can be approximated.

93
00:04:42.813 --> 00:04:45.510
Search only the combinations

94
00:04:45.606 --> 00:04:49.170
of a few trial vectors, x equals V y.

95
00:04:49.267 --> 00:04:51.868
The energy on that subspace

96
00:04:51.964 --> 00:04:53.987
gives a small system

97
00:04:53.987 --> 00:04:56.780
with matrix V transpose K V

98
00:04:56.780 --> 00:04:59.670
and right side V transpose f.

99
00:05:00.020 --> 00:05:03.016
For the springs, try one direction.

100
00:05:03.016 --> 00:05:06.867
Both masses moving together, along one, one

101
00:05:06.867 --> 00:05:09.264
gives P equal to minus two.

102
00:05:09.264 --> 00:05:13.116
Only the first mass moving, along one, zero

103
00:05:13.116 --> 00:05:15.940
gives minus two point six seven.

104
00:05:16.290 --> 00:05:19.053
Both are above the true minus three

105
00:05:19.130 --> 00:05:21.663
and the better guess of the shape

106
00:05:21.740 --> 00:05:23.352
has the lower energy.

107
00:05:23.352 --> 00:05:24.964
The direction three

108
00:05:24.964 --> 00:05:27.573
one points along the true answer

109
00:05:27.573 --> 00:05:30.490
and the Ritz method finds it exactly.

110
00:05:30.840 --> 00:05:33.133
The reason is the identity

111
00:05:33.218 --> 00:05:36.445
minimising P on the subspace minimises

112
00:05:36.530 --> 00:05:38.568
the energy of the error.

113
00:05:38.653 --> 00:05:41.030
Each line touches the lowest

114
00:05:41.115 --> 00:05:42.644
ring it can reach

115
00:05:42.729 --> 00:05:45.616
and the error ends up K orthogonal

116
00:05:45.701 --> 00:05:46.720
to the line.

117
00:05:47.300 --> 00:05:49.561
For a positive definite K

118
00:05:49.561 --> 00:05:51.560
solving is minimising

119
00:05:51.647 --> 00:05:53.994
and the extra energy is the

120
00:05:54.081 --> 00:05:55.820
energy of the error.

121
00:05:56.170 --> 00:06:00.620
Springs give K equals A transpose C A

122
00:06:00.620 --> 00:06:05.890
and the Ritz method minimises on a subspace.

123
00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:06.743
Next

124
00:06:06.743 --> 00:06:10.870
let the springs become a continuous bar

125
00:06:10.870 --> 00:06:15.400
and the Ritz method becomes finite elements.
